---
// /positioning — how a site's positioning is worked out before any copy, and how a test
// keeps each page on it. Positioning term (tests/positioning.spec.ts): "positioning",
// carried in the title, the description and the first paragraph.
// Spec: CONTENT_GUIDE.md, "Page `/positioning`". The page-and-term table is read
// from the test file at build time (src/data/positioning.ts); nothing in it is typed.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config';
import { SPINE } from '../data/positioning';
import { SUITE_COUNT } from '../data/suites';

const description =
  'How Webcroft works out your positioning before any copy: five questions after April Dunford, ' +
  'one file in your repo, and a test that keeps each page on it.';

// The first lines of a real failure of the positioning suite, for a page that lost its term.
const failure = [
  '/checks positioning not threaded (title="Our checks | Webcroft" · h1="How we check"):',
  '  • <title> needs "test gate"',
  '  • <h1>/intro needs "test gate"',
].join('\n');

const ownFile = 'https://github.com/karero/webcroft-site/blob/main/POSITIONING.md';

// Plain links, no tag. Checked by hand when the page was built (CONTENT_GUIDE.md).
const books = [
  {
    author: 'April Dunford', title: 'Obviously Awesome',
    role: 'The method behind the five questions.',
    own: { label: 'aprildunford.com', href: 'https://www.aprildunford.com/books' },
    asin: 'B0GLHYWFT9',
  },
  {
    author: 'April Dunford', title: 'Sales Pitch',
    role: 'Her second book, on the sales pitch. Further reading: Webcroft does not use it.',
    own: { label: 'aprildunford.com', href: 'https://www.aprildunford.com/books' },
    asin: 'B0CHY6BNDN',
  },
  {
    author: 'Donald Miller', title: 'Building a StoryBrand 2.0',
    role: 'The idea behind the optional story skill.',
    own: { label: 'storybrand.com', href: 'https://storybrand.com/building-a-storybrand-book-new/' },
    asin: 'B0CWTNCZCH',
  },
];
---
<Base title="Positioning first: what your site says" description={description} image="/images/og/positioning.jpg">
  <section class="wrap hero">
    <h1>Decide what your site says. Then build it.</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Before Webcroft writes a line of copy or picks a colour, it works out your
      positioning: what you offer, for whom, and what they would use instead. The
      answers go into one file in your repo, and a test keeps every page on them.
    </p>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn primary" href={SITE.repo}>Get Webcroft on GitHub</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/start">Quickstart</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="what">
    <h2>Positioning is the decision behind the copy</h2>
    <p>
      Positioning is not a slogan. It is a short set of decisions: who the site is
      for, what you offer them, what they would do if you did not exist, and which
      kind of thing you are. A visitor who recognises the kind of thing understands
      the value without a long explanation.
    </p>
    <p>
      It comes first because it decides what the copy is trying to say. Keywords,
      page titles, the tone of voice and the design all read these decisions. They
      do not make them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="questions">
    <h2>Five questions, asked in plain words</h2>
    <p>
      The assistant explains the idea in a few sentences, then asks. Where it can,
      it first looks at your customers and at what they use today, so the answers
      rest on facts. It asks in words like these:
    </p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><strong>Competitive alternatives.</strong> What would your customer use if you did not exist? Often that is a spreadsheet, a colleague or doing nothing, not a direct rival.</li>
      <li><strong>Unique attributes.</strong> What do you have that those alternatives do not? Facts, not adjectives.</li>
      <li><strong>Value and proof.</strong> What does each of those make possible for the customer, and what shows that it is true?</li>
      <li><strong>Target customer.</strong> Who cares a lot about that value? A group you can describe, not everyone.</li>
      <li><strong>Market category.</strong> Which kind of thing are you, so that the value is obvious? The assistant searches each candidate first. If the results show a different idea, that category would file your site in the wrong place.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">
      The method is April Dunford's, from her book <cite>Obviously Awesome</cite>.
      The five parts are hers. The questions, and the way a site is built on the
      answers, are Webcroft's. <a href="#sources">Sources</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="file">
    <h2>What you get: one file, and one term for each page</h2>
    <p>
      The answers land in a file called <code>POSITIONING.md</code> in your
      repository. It holds the five parts, a positioning statement of one
      paragraph, a description of about fifty words for search engines and AI
      assistants, and one term for each page: the thing that page wants to be known
      for.
    </p>
    <p>
      This site is built the same way. <a href={ownFile}>Its own positioning file</a>
      is public, and these are the terms its pages carry today:
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="The positioning term of each page of this site" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Page</th><th scope="col">Term</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {SPINE.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><a href={row.path}><code>{row.path}</code></a></th>
              <td>
                {row.term}
                {row.body.length > 0 && <span class="also"> (in the text also: {row.body.join('; ')})</span>}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      This table is read from the test file when the site is built. Nobody types
      it. The two legal pages carry no term.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="test">
    <h2>A test keeps every page on its term</h2>
    <p>
      Each term is also written into the positioning suite, one of the
      {SUITE_COUNT} test suites every site carries. For every page in its list the
      suite checks that the term appears in three places: the page title, the
      description for search results, and the main heading or the first paragraph.
      On the home page it also looks for the market category in the text.
    </p>
    <p>Rewrite a heading and lose the term, and the suite fails with a message like this:</p>
    <pre class="message"><code>{failure}</code></pre>
    <p>
      By default it runs before every push and again on GitHub, with the rest of
      <a href="/checks">the test gate</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="limits">
    <h2>What the test does not prove</h2>
    <ul class="steps">
      <li><strong>That the positioning is good.</strong> It proves that the term is there and the same everywhere. Whether it is the right term stays a judgment.</li>
      <li><strong>That more is better.</strong> It does not count words. The term said once, where it belongs, passes. Repeating it earns nothing.</li>
      <li><strong>That every page has a term.</strong> A page with no declared term only raises a warning.</li>
      <li><strong>That the decisions were the assistant's to make.</strong> It asks and drafts. You decide.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For a look from outside there is an optional skill,
      <code>website-positioning-check</code>: can a visitor tell what is on offer,
      for whom, and why to believe it?
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="trust">
    <h2>Then make it believable</h2>
    <p>
      Positioning says what you claim. The next step, the content guide, decides
      how the site sounds and why a reader should believe it. Google's name for
      those reasons is E-E-A-T, often written EEAT: experience, expertise,
      authoritativeness and trust. It is what Google's quality raters look for. It
      is not a score, and
      <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/creating-helpful-content">Google says</a>
      it is not a ranking factor by itself.
    </p>
    <p>The skills build these signals in, from the facts you supply:</p>
    <ul class="steps">
      <li>Structured data that names the organisation behind the site, with links to profiles that are really yours.</li>
      <li>A named author with a short bio, where a page has one.</li>
      <li>Sources, cited where a page makes claims.</li>
      <li>An imprint, a privacy page and a clear way to reach you.</li>
      <li>Links that work.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="aside">
      A test checks two of these: every page carries structured data that can be
      read, and links inside the site resolve when they are written from the site
      root. The rest is a checklist in your content guide. Whether a profile or
      another outside link still works is checked by hand or by a link audit. No
      test can tell whether a claim is true.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="story">
    <h2>Optional: the home page as your visitor's story</h2>
    <p>
      After positioning, the assistant offers one more step, once: the skill
      <code>website-story</code>. It tells the home page as the story of your
      visitor, in seven sections, and writes it down in a file called
      <code>STORY.md</code>. It is optional and never runs unasked. This site does
      not use it.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      The skill is inspired by Donald Miller's StoryBrand framework and puts the
      idea in Webcroft's own words. <a href="#sources">Sources</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="sources">
    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <dl class="books">
      {books.map((b) => (
        <div>
          <dt>{b.author}, <cite>{b.title}</cite></dt>
          <dd>
            {b.role}
            <span class="links">
              <a href={b.own.href}>{b.own.label}</a>
              <a href={`https://www.amazon.com/dp/${b.asin}`}>Amazon.com</a>
              <a href={`https://www.amazon.de/dp/${b.asin}`}>Amazon.de</a>
            </span>
          </dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>
    <p class="note">
      Webcroft is not affiliated with April Dunford or Donald Miller, and neither
      endorses it. StoryBrand is a trademark of its owner. These are plain links:
      Webcroft earns nothing from them. The Amazon links open the Kindle edition;
      the other formats are one click from there.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="next">
    <h2>See it on your own site</h2>
    <p>
      The quickstart takes you from nothing to a first site. Positioning is the
      second step of every build, right after a look at your customers and what
      they use today.
    </p>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn primary" href={SITE.repo}>Get Webcroft on GitHub</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/start">Quickstart</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  section.wrap { padding: 2.5rem 1.25rem; max-width: var(--maxw); margin: 0 auto; }
  section.hero { padding-top: 3rem; }
  .hero h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem); line-height: 1.1; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  h2 { margin-bottom: 0.8rem; line-height: 1.25; }
  section p, .steps { max-width: 46rem; line-height: 1.7; hyphens: manual; }
  .lead { font-size: 1.15rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .note { font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--muted); }
  code { font-size: 0.9em; }
  .steps { padding-left: 1.3rem; }
  .steps li { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }

  .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 1.2rem 0; max-width: 46rem; }
  table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; }
  th, td { text-align: left; vertical-align: top; padding: 0.6rem 0.9rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); line-height: 1.5; }
  thead th { font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--muted); }
  tbody th { font-weight: 400; white-space: nowrap; }
  .also { color: var(--muted); }

  /* The failure message is prose-width text: it wraps instead of scrolling. */
  .message { white-space: pre-wrap; overflow-wrap: anywhere; max-width: 46rem; }

  .books { margin: 1rem 0 0; max-width: 46rem; }
  .books > div { padding: 0.8rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); line-height: 1.6; }
  .books dt { font-weight: 600; }
  .books dd { margin: 0; color: var(--muted); }
  .links { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.2rem 1.2rem; margin-top: 0.3rem; }
</style>
